rgin": 13.2, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.17, "baseline_margin": 13.56, "analyst_adjustment": -0.56, "key_factors": ["Sykes holds a large cash advantage ($1.9M on hand vs. $78K for Coleman)", "Expert ratings cluster at Safe/Solid or Likely D across ten raters", "Prediction markets price Sykes around 96% to win", "District leans Democratic by about two points on the PVI"], "rationale": "The fundamentals and ratings both point to a comfortable Sykes win: she is a well-funded incumbent in a slightly Democratic-leaning district, while Coleman raised only about $127K and drew a contentious primary. Markets and raters agree the race is not competitive, so the forecast stays near the fundamentals-based margin.", "watch": "A late surge of outside Republican spending or an unexpectedly strong national GOP environment could narrow the margin, though flipping the seat remains a long shot.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Emilia Sykes (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Emilia Sykes seeks a third term in a northeastern Ohio district that leans slightly Democratic. She enters the general election with a decisive financial edge, nearly $1.9 million on hand, while Republican nominee Carey Coleman raised modestly in a five-way primary. An independent candidate, Sandeep Dixit, is on the ballot but has minimal resources.\n\nExpert raters uniformly place the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, with a few calling it Likely D, and prediction markets price Sykes at roughly 96 percent. With no public general-election polling available, the forecast rests on the district's lean, incumbency, and the funding gap, all of which favor Sykes by a wide margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9765, "margin": 13.33}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9641, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9651, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.965, "m